\title{Kernel async blocks}
\taxon{Design}
\date{2026-10-14}
\author{schrodingerzy}
\tag{design}
\meta{created}{2026-10-14}
\meta{updated}{2026-10-15}
\meta{origin}{docs/design/kernel-async.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\code{\verb~|async { ... }~} starts one eligible StableHLO operation inside a \code{\verb~|#[kernel]~} function. The result is \code{\verb~|core::intrinsic::tensor::Future<Tensor<...>>~}; \code{\verb~|future.done()~} obtains its tensor result when the core package is loaded. The member is an ordinary generic \code{\verb~|#[kernel]~} method in core whose body calls \code{\verb~|core::intrinsic::tensor::async_done(self)~}. The free intrinsic also works in bare compiler sessions without core declarations. The compiler emits \code{\verb~|stablehlo.async_start~} and \code{\verb~|stablehlo.async_done~}. These operations express a dependency; they do not guarantee concurrent execution.}

\pre{\verb~|
import core::intrinsic::tensor;

#[kernel]
fn begin(x: Tensor<[f32; 8]>, start: i32) -> Future<Tensor<[f32; 3]>> {
    async { tensor::dynamic_slice<Tensor<[f32; 3]>>(x, (start,)) }
}

#[kernel]
pub fn slice(x: Tensor<[f32; 8]>, start: i32) -> Tensor<[f32; 3]> {
    let pending = begin(x, start);
    pending.done()
}
~}

\p{The \code{\verb~|dynamic_slice<Result>(input, starts)~} intrinsic takes exactly one explicit result Tensor type. Input and result have the same rank and element type. The result shape must be static; known input dimensions must be at least as large as the corresponding slice sizes. Empty dimensions are legal. \code{\verb~|starts~} is a tuple with one integer per dimension: either source integers or rank-zero integer Tensors, all sharing one integer type. Rank-zero input uses \code{\verb~|()~}. StableHLO clamps start indices to the input bounds. Source integer scalars in kernels lower to rank-zero tensors; they are not host values.}

\p{\code{\verb~|slice<Result>(input, starts)~} is the static, unit-stride variant. Its start offsets are a tuple of nonnegative integer literals, checked as signed 64-bit attributes. They must fit within the input when its dimensions are known. Offsets are metadata and emit no operations. For example:}

\pre{\verb~|
#[kernel]
pub fn middle(x: Tensor<[f32; 8]>) -> Tensor<[f32; 3]> {
    tensor::async_done(async { tensor::slice<Tensor<[f32; 3]>>(x, (2,)) })
}
~}

\p{\code{\verb~|collective_permute(input, pairs)~} routes a tensor between replicas and returns the same Tensor type. \code{\verb~|pairs~} is a tuple of literal \code{\verb~|(source, target)~} replica ids, for example \code{\verb~|((0, 1), (1, 0))~}. Ids are nonnegative signed 64-bit integers; each source and each target must be unique. The empty tuple \code{\verb~|()~} routes no values, so unmatched replicas receive zero tensors. This API uses cross-replica routing without a channel handle; it does not invent a partition placement. The configured runtime replica count determines whether the ids are in range.}

\p{The async body must return the result of exactly one \code{\verb~|slice~}, \code{\verb~|dynamic_slice~} or \code{\verb~|collective_permute~} operation. Tuple construction, projection and local bindings can arrange its operands and result because they emit no operations. Bind runtime constants and arithmetic outside the block; static slice offset literals remain inside as attributes. Calls, conditionals, nested async blocks and \code{\verb~|async_done~} inside the block are rejected. This restriction follows the pinned StableHLO verifier, not a general promise that arbitrary kernel blocks can execute asynchronously.}

\p{Captures are explicit in HIR and MIR and become async operands and matching region block arguments. The region is isolated from outer SSA values. Captured products flatten to tensor leaves; Future, Token and host handles cannot be captured. Bind an individual tensor field outside the block if its containing product also carries such a value. No RC allocation or host closure represents an async block. Generic substitution and imported bodies repeat validation before lowering, including the exact body result and capture identities.}

\p{Future and Token remain kernel SSA types. Private kernel helpers may accept or return Future; a public PJRT entry must expose only tensor leaves. A host function cannot create an async block, call these intrinsics, or receive a Future from a kernel. Token is a side-effect ordering value, not a completion handle. This feature adds neither host futures nor a host launch API, and does not impose an extra source linearity rule on Future or Token.}

\p{The general Future type still supports several Tensor payloads. The pinned async operations support exactly one payload, so \code{\verb~|async_done~} rejects a multi-payload Future. Builds without OpenXLA can parse, type-check and round-trip these programs, but report the missing feature when asked to lower kernels.}

\subtree[design-kernel-async-specification-and-implementation-contract]{
\title{Specification and implementation contract}

\p{The reference is \<html:a>[href]{\verb~|https://openxla.org/stablehlo/spec#async_start~}{StableHLO async_start and async_done}, checked against revision \code{\verb~|b9029ac2228a12e176ea20e56f7d3dcb6bf8a019~}: \<html:a>[href]{\verb~|https://github.com/openxla/stablehlo/blob/b9029ac2228a12e176ea20e56f7d3dcb6bf8a019/stablehlo/dialect/StablehloOps.td~}{operation traits} and \<html:a>[href]{\verb~|https://github.com/openxla/stablehlo/blob/b9029ac2228a12e176ea20e56f7d3dcb6bf8a019/stablehlo/dialect/StablehloOps.cpp~}{verifiers and return-type inference}. The verifier permits one collective or slice operation plus its return, with Tensor operands matching region arguments exactly. This PR exposes static/dynamic slice and collective permutation source intrinsics; the other eligible operations do not acquire source APIs implicitly. See also the [existing Future/Token type design](design-stablehlo-async-types).}
}

\subtree[design-kernel-async-tested-backend-boundaries]{
\title{Tested backend boundaries}

\p{The installed CPU PJRT plugin's \<html:a>[href]{\verb~|https://github.com/openxla/xla/blob/fc840b37e26bd36c99f32e295324a1700dc12ecd/xla/hlo/translate/mhlo_to_hlo/mlir_hlo_to_hlo.cc~}{XLA importer} supports only one-operand collective producers in \code{\verb~|async_start~}; it rejects slices even though the pinned StableHLO verifier permits them. It also requires an \code{\verb~|async_done~} operand to be defined directly by \code{\verb~|async_start~}, so Future values passed through helper calls remain valid compiler/StableHLO IR but are not accepted by this importer. Codegen always inlines straight-line wrappers containing one intrinsic, including the ordinary \code{\verb~|Future.done~} member, before emitting MLIR. This does not require an optimization level or an extra pass. Arguments are evaluated once in source order; the callee keeps its own variable and ownership frame. Calls, control flow, closures, regional functions, and explicit transform anchors are outside this simple-wrapper rule. More complex kernel helpers still use the existing MLIR inliner (\code{\verb~|reussir-opt --inline --symbol-dce~}) before PJRT import. No second completion intrinsic or method-name special case is introduced.}

\p{The StableHLO specification and pinned verifier do not prohibit repeated \code{\verb~|async_done~} uses. The current \<html:a>[href]{\verb~|https://openxla.org/xla/async_ops~}{XLA async contract} requires exactly one user for an async-start result, and the CPU PJRT compiler rejects two completions of the same future. Call \code{\verb~|done()~} once and reuse the returned Tensor wherever needed. This backend restriction is not a claim of StableHLO undefined behavior, and this change adds no source consumption rule.}

\p{The execution regression uses actual collective permutations, ordinary \code{\verb~|done()~} calls in a while body, and checks both delivered tensors and zero results for unmatched replicas.}

\p{Rank-zero dynamic slice has no indices and is equivalent to rank-zero static slice. Lowering uses the latter form because the pinned StableHLO custom printer emits an extra comma for the former. Both remain genuine eligible operations inside the async region. Source→HIR→MIR→MLIR textual round trips cover this case.}
}
